---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Frameworks.astro';
import { FRAMEWORK_PAGES } from '../../_data/frameworks.ts';
---

<Layout
  title='Frameworks'
  description='Peacock components are custom elements, so they work in any framework. The framework packages add what each framework needs on top: typed props and events, two-way binding for fields, and registration that works with any bundler.'
>
  <section class='foundation-section' aria-labelledby='packages-title'>
    <h2 class='text-headline-small' id='packages-title'>Packages</h2>
    <p class='text-body-large'>
      Each package is generated from <code class='text-code-block'>custom-elements.json</code>, so it
      always matches the components of the same Peacock version. Install it next to
      <code class='text-code-block'>@redvars/peacock</code>.
    </p>
    <ul class='text-body-large'>
      {FRAMEWORK_PAGES.slice(1).map(page => (
        <li><a href={page.href}>{page.label}</a>: {page.description}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class='foundation-section' aria-labelledby='registration-title'>
    <h2 class='text-headline-small' id='registration-title'>Registration</h2>
    <p class='text-body-large'>
      Importing a component from a framework package defines its element, along with every
      Peacock element it renders, such as <code class='text-code-block'>wc-option</code> and
      <code class='text-code-block'>wc-icon</code> for a select. You do not need
      <code class='text-code-block'>loader.js</code>, and with React and Vue only the components
      you import end up in your bundle. Load the styles once, for example
      <code class='text-code-block'>import '@redvars/peacock/styles.css'</code>.
    </p>
    <p class='text-body-large'>
      Use one copy of Peacock per page. If <code class='text-code-block'>loader.js</code> or another
      copy has already defined a tag, the existing definition stays and Peacock warns once in the
      console.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class='foundation-section' aria-labelledby='ssr-title'>
    <h2 class='text-headline-small' id='ssr-title'>Server rendering</h2>
    <p class='text-body-large'>
      The components need a browser. With server rendering (Next.js, Nuxt, Angular SSR), render
      Peacock components on the client only.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>
